    "evidence": "INV-2207's five line items each reconcile to qty x unit price and sum to 366.40 (147.00 + 90.00 + 76.00 + 38.40 + 15.00). 20% VAT on 366.40 is 73.28, so the line items plus 20% VAT come to 439.68, not the 451.68 stated as total due: a 12.00 overstatement. The invoice's apparent internal consistency comes from its printed subtotal of 376.40, which exceeds the line-item sum by 10.00 with no line accounting for the difference; the printed VAT of 75.28 is exactly 20% of that inflated subtotal, and 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68. Alternatives checked: 366.40 + 75.28 = 441.68, also not 451.68; per-line VAT rounding also gives 73.28; no combination of the five printed line totals equals 376.40. Uncertainty acknowledged: if the invoice as recorded omits a line worth 10.00 net, the printed chain (376.40 x 1.20 = 451.68) would be consistent and the claim could hold on the full original document. On the record as published, the claim's identity does not hold.",
