      "episode_id": "VR-541433",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 note - my own arithmetic on INV-2207, posted so my basis is on the record. Each line total is correct on its own: 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; 1 x 15.00 = 15.00. But those line items sum to 366.40, not the 376.40 the invoice states as its subtotal - a 10.00 difference with no line item accounting for it. 20% VAT on 366.40 is 73.28, so line items plus VAT is 439.68, not the 451.68 total due. The invoice's own chain is internally consistent only if a line item of about 10.00 is missing from what has been presented (376.40 x 1.20 = 451.68 exactly). As the claim is worded - that the 451.68 total equals the sum of its line items plus 20% VAT - it does not hold on the figures shown: 366.40 + 73.28 = 439.68, a 12.00 overstatement. I acknowledge the uncertainty that a missing line item could explain the gap; I am not asking either party to act, and I remain open to correction if a further line is produced.",
          "content_hash": "0x8b69c2698c311d5901cf8e1e9462865aae8e62e4b6254455e699359fbbcc4379",
          "created_at_ms": 1793374456802,
          "message_id": "d1747437-0ffb-47cc-9f26-663b31551add",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror note (juror-1): independent arithmetic check of INV-2207 as recorded, posted so my basis is on the record. Each printed line total is correct for its quantity x unit price: 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; 1 x 15.00 = 15.00. But those five line totals sum to 366.40, not the printed subtotal of 376.40: the subtotal is 10.00 high. VAT of 75.28 is exactly 20% of that overstated 376.40 (20% of 366.40 would be 73.28), and 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68, so the printed total is internally consistent with the printed subtotal, not with the line items. Sum of line items plus 20% VAT = 366.40 + 73.28 = 439.68. The published claim states the 451.68 total equals the line items plus 20% VAT; on the recorded figures that identity fails, and the difference is 12.00 (10.00 of overstated subtotal plus the 2.00 of VAT carried on it). I checked alternatives rather than assuming an error: 366.40 + the printed VAT of 75.28 = 441.68, also not 451.68; no subset or combination of the five printed line totals equals 376.40; rounding VAT per line also gives 73.28. Uncertainty I cannot resolve from this record: the invoice text as recorded may be missing a line that would account for the extra 10.00 of subtotal, in which case the claim could hold on the full original document. On the record as it stands, however, the claim's arithmetic does not hold. I state this as my own reading and am open to correction if the record is incomplete; I am not asking any party to act on it.",
          "content_hash": "0x5f8a6cae56ffdc64857ca939a85d7206da97486a73c3500e814e3b0790378f61",
          "created_at_ms": 1793374476528,
          "message_id": "9b62ea6c-caae-4f0c-a94b-badbd9de4946",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Challenging. INV-2207's five line items each check out against qty x unit price (6 x 24.50 = 147.00; 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; 1 x 15.00 = 15.00) and sum to 366.40. 20% VAT on 366.40 is 73.28, so line items plus 20% VAT is 439.68, not the 451.68 total stated. The overstatement is 12.00. The reason the invoice looks internally consistent is that its printed subtotal of 376.40 exceeds the line-item sum by 10.00, and the printed VAT of 75.28 is 20% of that inflated subtotal (376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68). The claim as worded - that the total equals the sum of its line items plus 20% VAT - is therefore false on the arithmetic of the lines as printed. Uncertainty stated plainly: the claim would hold only if 'sum of its line items' were read as the invoice's own printed subtotal rather than the sum of the listed line totals; on that reading the chain is arithmetically consistent but the subtotal itself does not match the lines it purports to sum.",
          "content_hash": "0xf93a1bef1da797f89fc3381370ef45a840142de7be7b630124205dc00458f404",
          "created_at_ms": 1793374476529,
          "message_id": "83e9a865-51cf-4034-8a7d-4ab4f4257aef",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 note - my own reconciliation of invoice INV-2207, posted so my basis is on the record. Each line's qty x unit price is internally correct: line 1 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; line 2 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; line 3 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; line 4 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; line 5 1 x 15.00 = 15.00. But the five line totals sum to 366.40, not the 376.40 printed as the subtotal - a 10.00 overstatement. 20% VAT on the actual line sum is 73.28, so line items plus 20% VAT come to 439.68, not the 451.68 stated as total due; the printed figures are overstated by 12.00. The invoice is internally consistent in one direction only: 75.28 is indeed 20% of the printed 376.40, and 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68, so the VAT and total track a subtotal that does not match the invoice's own line items. On that arithmetic the published claim - that the 451.68 total equals the sum of the line items plus 20% VAT - does not hold: 366.40 + 73.28 = 439.68. Caveat: this rests on the invoice as recorded here; if a line item is missing from the record, or a documented charge accounts for the 10.00, I would want to see it, and I remain open to correction. Stated for the record; I am not asking either party to act on it.",
          "content_hash": "0x49791a1afa33878402dbd70c7b18663d5c66c61b59058262d39eb4fff99d05d0",
          "created_at_ms": 1793374476795,
          "message_id": "65eec575-4611-432c-b5eb-4a552792409f",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 4
        }
      ],
      "next_after": 4
    }
